>
  <div class="docs-article">
    <h1>Documentation</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The <a href="/spec/">specification</a> sets the normative requirements. This handbook shows
      how to apply them: how to publish and link an event file, how apps handle feeds, provenance,
      and missing information, and where the <code>0.1</code> working draft stops.
    </p>
    <nav class="companion-links" aria-label="Start by task or reference">
      <strong>On this page:</strong>
      <a href="#publish-your-first-event">Publish an event</a>
      <a href="#refresh-and-provenance">Feeds and refresh</a>
      <a href="#why-another-format">Evaluate the design</a>
      <span class="companion-sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <strong>Elsewhere:</strong>
      <a href="/spec/">Specification</a>
      <a href="/feeds/">Atom feed profile</a>
      <a href="/conversions/">Conversion guide</a>
      <a href="/validator/">Validator</a>
      <a href="/examples/">Examples</a>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="publish-your-first-event">Publish your first event</h2>
    <p class="audience">For organizers and publishers</p>

    <h3>1. Write the event document</h3>
    <p>
      Save your event as a UTF-8 JSON file (for example, <code>event.openajang.json</code>). The
      required fields are <code>$schema</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>url</code>,
      <code>title</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>starts_at</code>, <code>location</code>,
      <code>organizer</code>, and <code>updated_at</code>
      (<a href="/spec/#32-core-properties">Spec §3.2</a>). Use the exact <code>$schema</code> URL
      for version <code>0.1</code>, and leave out optional fields you do not have instead of
      setting them to <code>null</code> or empty strings. The sample also includes the optional
      <code>id</code>, <code>summary</code>, <code>ends_at</code>, <code>time_zone</code>, and
      <code>registration</code> fields:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`{
  "$schema": "https://openajang.nedpals.xyz/schema/0.1/event.json",
  "type": "event",
  "id": "urn:uuid:514f2697-c7d6-4fa0-975b-8c682a5dcf87",
  "url": "https://example.org/events/build-night-2026",
  "title": "Community Build Night",
  "summary": "An evening of building and sharing projects in Davao.",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "starts_at": "2026-11-14T18:00:00+08:00",
  "ends_at": "2026-11-14T20:00:00+08:00",
  "time_zone": "Asia/Manila",
  "organizer": {
    "name": "Example Community"
  },
  "location": {
    "type": "physical",
    "physical": {
      "name": "Community Hall, Davao City"
    }
  },
  "registration": {
    "required": true,
    "status": "open",
    "url": "https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/EXAMPLE/viewform"
  },
  "updated_at": "2026-10-29T08:00:00Z"
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Here, <code>url</code> is the canonical human-readable event page (such as your website or a
      repository README), while the JSON file itself can be hosted at a separate
      <a href="/spec/#2-terminology">Document URL</a>. Publish only public event information: keep
      attendee details, credentials, personalized ticket links, and private meeting access URLs out
      of the document. Check your file in the <a href="/validator/">browser validator</a> before
      publishing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <code>id</code> is optional. Without one, <code>url</code> identifies the event, so it
      has to stay stable. Generate a UUID once and set <code>id</code> if <code>url</code> is a
      homepage, a mutable slug, or a third-party page that may move
      (<a href="/spec/#51-identity">Spec §5.1</a>). Never reuse an <code>id</code> for another
      occurrence.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Host with an accepted JSON media type</h3>
    <p>
      Serve the file over HTTPS with <code>Content-Type: application/vnd.openajang+json</code> or
      <code>application/json</code> (parameters such as <code>charset=utf-8</code> are allowed).
      Confirm what your host actually sends, for example with
      <code>curl -I https://example.org/event.openajang.json</code>. The validator checks the
      document, not the hosted response.
    </p>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Common hosting pitfall:</strong> Committing a file to a Git repository does not by
        itself create a conforming endpoint. A normal GitHub file-view URL returns an HTML page,
        and many raw repository views serve <code>text/plain</code>. Publish through GitHub Pages
        or another host that serves an accepted JSON media type, and enable CORS
        (<code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code>) if you want browser-only tools to fetch your
        document directly.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>3. Link the JSON from your HTML event page (optional)</h3>
    <p>
      If you control the HTML of the page at <code>url</code>, add an alternate link inside its
      <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`<link
  rel="alternate"
  type="application/vnd.openajang+json"
  href="./event.openajang.json"
/>`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Discovery follows strict rules (<a href="/spec/#82-html-discovery">Spec §8.2</a>) so that
      independent resolvers always reach the same answer:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The <code>&lt;link&gt;</code> must be present in the initial HTML <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> response without executing JavaScript.</li>
      <li>Relative <code>href</code> values resolve against the final fetched HTML URL, <strong>ignoring any HTML <code>&lt;base&gt;</code> element</strong>.</li>
      <li>Multiple identical links are deduplicated, but two links pointing to different JSON URLs fail with an ambiguity error rather than guessing.</li>
      <li>The linked URL must return the JSON document directly; recursive HTML-to-HTML discovery is forbidden.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>4. Keep the event’s identity stable</h3>
    <p>
      Apps track an event by its <a href="/spec/#2-terminology">Effective Event ID</a>: the
      <code>id</code> field if present, or <code>url</code> when <code>id</code> is omitted.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        If you published without an <code>id</code> and later move the event page to a new
        <code>url</code>, set <code>id</code> to the <em>previous <code>url</code></em>, not a
        freshly generated UUID. That keeps the event continuous for everyone who already saved it.
      </li>
      <li>
        When an event is delayed and the new time is not yet confirmed, set <code>status</code> to
        <code>"postponed"</code>, keep the old <code>starts_at</code>, and explain in
        <code>status_note</code>. Once a new time is confirmed, set <code>status</code> to
        <code>"rescheduled"</code>, update <code>starts_at</code> and <code>ends_at</code>, and
        append the replaced start time to <code>previous_starts_at</code> in the order the changes
        happened.
      </li>
      <li>
        When an event is cancelled, keep the JSON endpoint live and return HTTP
        <code>200 OK</code> with <code>status: "cancelled"</code> and a newer
        <code>updated_at</code>, so people who saved the event see the cancellation. HTTP
        <code>410 Gone</code> means you removed the document itself, and apps do not read that as a
        cancellation.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="refresh-and-provenance">Feeds, provenance, and refresh</h2>
    <p class="audience">For app and directory developers</p>
    <p>
      People can bring events into a compatible app either by pasting a URL or by subscribing to a
      directory’s <a href="/feeds/">Atom feed</a>. In both cases the app keeps track of where each
      claim came from:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Supplied and final fetched URL:</strong> the URL given to the resolver and its final target after redirects.</li>
      <li><strong>Document URL:</strong> the HTTPS endpoint where the JSON was retrieved.</li>
      <li><strong>Declared Event URL (<code>url</code>):</strong> the human-facing page claimed inside the JSON document.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When discovery starts from an HTML Event URL, the linked JSON’s <code>url</code> should match
      that page (or its canonical head link). When a JSON URL is imported directly or linked from a
      third-party directory feed, a declared <code>url</code> on another origin is an unverified
      claim unless the page at <code>url</code> also links back to that JSON. Apps should keep
      directory provenance separate from publisher provenance, and should not let an unrelated host
      that copied an Event ID overwrite a known event
      (<a href="/spec/#51-identity">Spec §5.1</a>,
      <a href="/spec/#83-redirects-caching-and-refresh">§8.3</a>).
    </p>

    <h3>Following directory feeds</h3>
    <p>
      A directory feed links to publisher-hosted event documents instead of replacing them. The
      <a href="/feeds/">Atom feed profile</a> sets four rules for apps that follow one:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Entry and document identity must match:</strong> after fetching the linked
        <code>application/vnd.openajang+json</code> document, the app checks that the document’s
        Effective Event ID exactly equals the Atom entry <code>&lt;id&gt;</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Feed omission is not cancellation or deletion:</strong> directories may cap feed
        length, filter by topic, or age out older listings. When an event disappears from a feed,
        the app keeps the saved event and keeps refreshing its Document URL directly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bounded feeds cannot guarantee cancellation delivery:</strong> an entry may leave a
        bounded feed before it is cancelled, so apps rely on direct Document URL refreshes to learn
        about late changes and cancellations.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Three distinct clocks:</strong> the Atom entry <code>&lt;updated&gt;</code>
        timestamp, the document’s <code>updated_at</code>, and the app’s own HTTP retrieval time
        measure different things and are not interchangeable.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Refreshing a saved Document URL</h3>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Refresh outcome</th>
          <th scope="col">What an app that stores events does (<a href="/spec/#83-redirects-caching-and-refresh">Spec §8.3</a>)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HTTP <code>200</code>, valid JSON, same ID, and the source is still the one the event came from</th>
          <td>Replace the stored representation and record the retrieval time. Warn if the content changed without advancing <code>updated_at</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HTTP <code>304 Not Modified</code></th>
          <td>Keep the existing valid representation.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HTTP <code>200</code>, invalid JSON or validation error</th>
          <td>Retain the last valid representation and record the refresh failure.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HTTP <code>200</code>, different Effective Event ID</th>
          <td>Do not overwrite the saved event (commonly happens when an organizer reuses a URL for a new edition without keeping the old document separate).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HTTP <code>404</code> on a discovered Document URL</th>
          <td>Repeat HTML discovery from the original Event URL in case the publisher moved the JSON file path; otherwise apply removal policy.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">HTTP <code>410 Gone</code></th>
          <td>Apply document-removal policy; do not report the event as cancelled unless a valid cancelled representation was received.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Network error or HTTP <code>5xx</code></th>
          <td>Retain the last valid representation and retry with backoff.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="occurrence-boundaries">Occurrence boundaries</h2>
    <p class="audience">For publishers and app developers</p>
    <p>
      Every OpenAjang document represents a single identified event occurrence (such as a monthly
      community meetup) or one overall event edition (such as an annual three-day conference or a
      four-month museum exhibition). In practice:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A date range is the edition’s outer calendar envelope, not a claim of continuous activity.
        A multi-day conference or exhibition need not run every day or around the clock. Put
        visiting hours and daily schedules in <code>description</code> or
        <code>instructions</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>location.type</code> set to <code>"hybrid"</code> means physical and virtual
        participation both exist somewhere in the event, not that every day, workshop, or ticket
        tier offers both. State important attendance limits in core text fields even when an
        extension adds structure.
      </li>
      <li>
        An in-person conference that posts recordings afterward stays <code>"physical"</code>
        unless live remote participation is part of the event.
      </li>
      <li>
        Workshops, hackathons, or side events within a larger edition can be listed as plain-text
        ticket options on the overall document, or published as separate documents with their own
        Event IDs. An overall listing and a component are not duplicates just because their dates
        overlap, and sharing a <code>series</code> object does not imply shared admission.
      </li>
      <li>
        If a conference reuses the same homepage URL every year (for example,
        <code>https://example.org/conf</code>), give each edition its own permanent
        <code>id</code>, such as a UUID URN or a year-specific URI
        (<a href="/spec/#51-identity">Spec §5.1</a>). Reusing one identity makes apps overwrite last
        year’s edition as if it had been rescheduled.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="conservative-interpretation">Conservative interpretation</h2>
    <p class="audience">For app developers</p>
    <p>
      A core design rule in <a href="/spec/#11-occurrence-boundaries-and-conservative-interpretation">Section 1.1 of the specification</a>
      is that consumers must never invent precision, availability, or permissions that the
      publisher did not state. When data is omitted or partial, consumers must surface that
      uncertainty honestly.
    </p>

    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Published data</th>
          <th scope="col">Required consumer interpretation</th>
          <th scope="col">What consumers must not do</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Date-only <code>starts_at</code> / <code>ends_at</code> (<code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>)</th>
          <td>Treat <code>ends_at</code> as the inclusive last calendar date and indicate that exact clock hours are not published.</td>
          <td>Do not assume midnight (<code>00:00</code>), convert to an all-day busy calendar block, or mix date-only and timestamp precision.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Timed <code>starts_at</code> with optional <code>time_zone</code></th>
          <td>Treat the RFC 3339 UTC offset (for example, <code>+08:00</code>) as the authoritative instant; use the IANA <code>time_zone</code> for local display context.</td>
          <td>Do not silently shift the encoded instant if local timezone rules or offsets disagree; warn if needed and preserve the instant.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Event <code>status: "scheduled"</code> after end time passes</th>
          <td>Classify the event as past based on its timestamps (and <code>time_zone</code> context for date-only events).</td>
          <td>Do not require organizers to manually flip every past event to <code>"completed"</code>, and do not show an ended event as upcoming.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>registration.status</code> values</th>
          <td>When a registration <code>url</code> exists, show <strong>Register</strong> only for <code>"open"</code>, <strong>Join waitlist</strong> for <code>"waitlist"</code>, and <strong>View registration</strong> for <code>"unknown"</code> or omitted status.</td>
          <td>Do not encourage registration when status is <code>"closed"</code> or <code>"not_yet_open"</code> (show that state instead; a plain source link may stay), or when the event is <code>cancelled</code>, <code>postponed</code>, or <code>completed</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>tickets</code> and <code>price</code></th>
          <td>Preserve tier descriptions, eligibility notes, and exact decimal amounts. Treat <code>"0"</code> as free for that option and omitted <code>price</code> as unknown.</td>
          <td>Do not infer that an omitted price is free, compute an unrestricted “starting at” lowest price across restricted tiers, or claim a ticket covers every event component.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Unannounced physical venue (<code>status: "to_be_announced"</code>)</th>
          <td>Display known city, region, or country context from <code>address</code> while noting the venue is to be announced.</td>
          <td>Do not put placeholder strings like <code>"TBA"</code> into <code>physical.name</code> (which validation rejects).</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="why-another-format">Why another format?</h2>
    <p class="audience">For anyone evaluating the design</p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://schema.org/Event">Schema.org Event</a>,
      <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5545">iCalendar (RFC 5545)</a>, and
      <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8984">JSCalendar (RFC 8984)</a> already cover much
      of this information. OpenAjang tests a smaller prescribed authoring and URL-import contract:
      one JSON object shape for a single public event occurrence, explicit registration fields, and
      strict rules for HTML discovery and saved-event refresh.
    </p>
    <p>
      Simpler authoring is a hypothesis to test with organizers. Publishing tools should generate
      the event page and compatibility outputs from one record, not ask organizers to maintain a
      second copy.
    </p>

    <h3>How OpenAjang relates to existing formats</h3>
    <p>
      OpenAjang is designed as the editable source document. Compatible consumers and directories
      read OpenAjang JSON directly; they do not need to convert it into Schema.org or JSCalendar
      before importing or distributing it. When compatibility outputs are generated, semantic
      differences and losses are reported explicitly rather than hidden.
    </p>

    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Format</th>
          <th scope="col">Role in an OpenAjang workflow</th>
          <th scope="col">Key trade-offs and conversion boundaries</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">OpenAjang (<code>event.openajang.json</code>)</th>
          <td>Editable source published at a stable URL for direct import and directory refresh.</td>
          <td>Prescribes one JSON object shape, explicit registration states, and occurrence lifecycle rules. Does not model recurring rules or room-by-room session schedules.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Schema.org Event (JSON-LD)</th>
          <td>Generated output for web pages and search indexing (<a href="/conversions/#1-schemaorg-json-ld">mapping rules</a>).</td>
          <td>Preserves date or date-time precision. Registration maps to a documented <code>potentialAction</code> convention rather than a universal protocol; <code>completed</code> has no exact lifecycle match; generated markup does not guarantee search engine eligibility.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">JSCalendar (RFC 8984)</th>
          <td>Generated output for timed calendar events (<a href="/conversions/#2-jscalendar">mapping rules</a>).</td>
          <td>Normalizes timed instants to UTC and computes exact elapsed duration across offset transitions. Conversions are <strong>explicitly declined</strong> when clock times are unpublished (date-only events), when <code>ends_at</code> is omitted, or when an event is <code>postponed</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">iCalendar (<code>.ics</code>, RFC 5545)</th>
          <td>Not generated by the <code>0.1</code> reference tools (<a href="/conversions/#3-icalendar-and-reminders">notes</a>).</td>
          <td>A user-created calendar reminder for a date-only event is a separate user action and must never be advertised as a lossless event export.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="what-openajang-does-not-do">What OpenAjang does not do</h2>
    <p class="audience">For anyone evaluating the design</p>
    <p>
      OpenAjang is a small contract between independent publishers and consumers, so several things
      that full event platforms or calendar suites handle are intentionally out of scope:
    </p>
    <dl class="boundary-list">
      <div>
        <dt>No automatic scraping or platform extraction</dt>
        <dd>
          Compatibility requires publishers to expose an OpenAjang JSON document. It does not
          automatically extract events from Facebook, Meetup, Eventbrite, or arbitrary HTML pages,
          and an invalid JSON response is an error, not an invitation to scrape the page.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>No central account or hosted registry</dt>
        <dd>
          There is no central OpenAjang service to join. Organizers host documents on their own
          website or static host (such as GitHub Pages). Directories are optional, and multiple
          directories can index the same publisher independently.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>No checkout, form submission, or live inventory guarantees</dt>
        <dd>
          OpenAjang links participants to the organizer’s existing registration form or ticket page
          (such as Google Forms, Luma, Tito, or a custom page). It does not submit registrations,
          collect attendee data, or guarantee real-time seat availability. A form can close or sell
          out between JSON updates.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>No organizer identity verification or directory endorsement</dt>
        <dd>
          Control of a host establishes the provenance of its claims, not that the publisher is the
          official event organizer. Passing validation means a document matches the schema and
          procedural checks; it does not verify organizer authority or guarantee inclusion in any
          directory.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>No recurrence expansion, opening-hours rules, or session schedules</dt>
        <dd>
          One document describes one event occurrence or one overall edition. The top-level
          <code>schedule</code> property is reserved and must be absent. Weekly visiting hours,
          recurring meeting rules, and multi-track conference agendas are outside the core contract.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>No inline HTML data blocks or executable content</dt>
        <dd>
          HTML discovery uses a <code>&lt;link rel="alternate" type="application/vnd.openajang+json"&gt;</code>
          tag in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> pointing to a standalone JSON URL. Inline script blocks
          are outside the discovery contract, and all display strings in an event document are
          plain text, never rendered as HTML.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="extensions">Extensions and core fallbacks</h2>
    <p>
      Publishers can include custom or experimental data inside the top-level
      <code>extensions</code> object using namespaced keys matching <code>namespace:name</code>
      (third-party namespaces should use reverse-domain notation such as
      <code>org.example:field</code>). The prefix <code>unstable:</code> is reserved for documented
      OpenAjang experiments.
    </p>
    <p>
      The governing rule is that <strong>stripping all extensions must leave an accurate, useful
      core listing</strong>. An extension can add structured detail for specialized tools, but it
      cannot contradict core fields, and the listing cannot depend on it to avoid a false
      impression. Broad implementation of an extension needs a separately reviewed contract and
      schema, and no generic validator may require support for specific extension keys. The
      <a href="/examples/">eight example fixtures</a> use five experimental keys, documented in
      <a href="https://github.com/nedpals/openajang/blob/master/experimental-extensions.md"><code>experimental-extensions.md</code></a>:
    </p>

    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Experimental key</th>
          <th scope="col">Illustrative structure</th>
          <th scope="col">Required core fallback in examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>unstable:components</code></th>
          <td>Array of sub-events with <code>name</code>, <code>starts_at</code>, and location-shaped <code>location</code>.</td>
          <td>Core <code>description</code> and venue/registration instructions state which days use which attendance modes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>unstable:eligibility</code></th>
          <td>Object with integer <code>minimum_age</code> and <code>maximum_age</code>.</td>
          <td>Registration <code>instructions</code> state age requirements (such as ages 18–26 for the Belgrade hackathon fixture).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>unstable:participation</code></th>
          <td>Object with team size bounds, <code>individual_matching_available</code>, and text <code>prerequisite</code>.</td>
          <td>Registration <code>instructions</code> describe team sizes, solo matching, and prerequisites in plain text.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>unstable:event_languages</code></th>
          <td>Object with <code>working</code> and <code>presentations</code> arrays of BCP 47 language tags.</td>
          <td>Core <code>description</code> names the spoken and presentation languages, while top-level <code>language</code> tags the JSON document’s own text.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><code>unstable:broadcast</code></th>
          <td>Object with <code>recordings_url</code> and boolean <code>counts_as_attendance</code>.</td>
          <td>Core <code>description</code> notes that recordings are posted while core <code>location.type</code> stays <code>"physical"</code>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="sdk-and-local-tools">SDKs, tools, and examples</h2>
    <p class="audience">For app developers</p>
    <p>
      You do not need to install any OpenAjang package to publish or consume event documents. Any
      strict JSON parser, schema validator, and HTTP client that implements the specification rules
      is enough. For JavaScript and TypeScript work and for local testing, the repository ships
      optional packages and Python reference tools.
    </p>

    <h3>TypeScript packages (<code>@openajang/core</code> and <code>@openajang/resolver</code>)</h3>
    <p>
      Both packages are release candidates for testing and feedback (requiring Node.js 22.18 or
      newer). Their APIs may change before <code>0.1.0</code>.
    </p>
    <pre><code>npm install @openajang/core@next @openajang/resolver@next</code></pre>
    <p>
      Use <a href="https://github.com/nedpals/openajang/tree/master/packages/core"><code>@openajang/core</code></a>
      for strict JSON parsing (rejecting duplicate keys and invalid Unicode), schema and procedural
      validation, and optional Schema.org / JSCalendar adapters. Adapter calls return
      <code>{`{ result, diagnostics }`}</code> and set <code>result</code> to <code>null</code> when
      a conversion is declined:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`import { parseJsonStrict, validateSafe } from '@openajang/core';
import { toSchemaOrg, toJSCalendar } from '@openajang/core/adapters';

const parsed = parseJsonStrict(rawJsonText);
const validation = validateSafe(parsed);
if (validation.valid) {
  const schemaOrg = toSchemaOrg(validation.event);
  const jsCalendar = toJSCalendar(validation.event);
  if (jsCalendar.result === null) {
    console.warn('JSCalendar conversion declined:', jsCalendar.diagnostics);
  }
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Use <a href="https://github.com/nedpals/openajang/tree/master/packages/resolver"><code>@openajang/resolver</code></a>
      in Node.js to fetch a direct JSON URL or discover an event document from an HTML page with
      bounded redirects, size limits, and private-IP blocking:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`import { resolveOpenAjang } from '@openajang/resolver';

const { event, provenance, relationshipWarnings } = await resolveOpenAjang(
  'https://example.org/events/build-night-2026'
);`}</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Known SDK limits: the schema uses custom <code>oa-*</code> format annotations that generic
        JSON Schema tools ignore unless paired with procedural checks. Time-zone checks in
        <code>@openajang/core</code> rely on the runtime’s JavaScript <code>Intl</code> data, JSON
        numbers use IEEE 754 precision, and full validation equivalence between the Python
        reference validator and the TypeScript SDK is still work in progress.
        <code>@openajang/resolver</code> performs one-shot retrieval, so applications that store
        events need to add conditional caching, source continuity checks across revisions, and
        their own production SSRF and network isolation testing.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Python reference CLI and conformance suite</h3>
    <p>
      From a clone of the <a href="https://github.com/nedpals/openajang">OpenAjang repository</a>
      with Python 3.11+, you can validate documents, regenerate the derived Schema.org and
      JSCalendar outputs under <code>generated/</code>, and run the offline regression suite:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`python -m venv .venv
. .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
python tools/openajang.py validate examples/*.openajang.json
python tools/openajang.py generate
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v`}</code></pre>

    <h3 id="example-provenance-and-testing">Example provenance and test coverage</h3>
    <p>
      The eight event files in <a href="/examples/">Examples</a> and the sample Atom and RSS feeds
      are illustrative snapshots built from supplied real-event data for conformance testing. See
      <a href="https://github.com/nedpals/openajang/blob/master/PUBLISHING.md"><code>PUBLISHING.md</code></a>
      for provenance and
      <a href="https://github.com/nedpals/openajang/blob/master/TEST-REPORT.md"><code>TEST-REPORT.md</code></a>
      for test results.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Event names, dates, URLs, and ticket tiers have not been rechecked against live organizer
        websites, and the fixtures do not show that any organizer authorized the listing or
        supports OpenAjang discovery. Each fixture names <code>Example fixture publisher</code>
        with a fixed revision instant of <code>2026-10-29T08:00:00Z</code>, and feed document URLs
        use <code>example.org</code> placeholders.
      </li>
      <li>
        Past events in the fixture set keep <code>status: "scheduled"</code> on purpose, to test
        that apps classify past events by date. Five of the eight fixtures use date-only envelopes
        and the JSCalendar converter declines them, with per-file notes in
        <a href="/generated/conversion-diagnostics.json"><code>conversion-diagnostics.json</code></a>.
        Passing the repository’s conformance suite verifies local reference behavior. It is not
        independent interoperability testing or certification by a target validator.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <p class="audience">Mostly for organizers and publishers</p>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details>
        <summary>Do I need to run a server or install the SDK to publish an event?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Any static <code>.json</code> file served over HTTPS with an accepted JSON media type
          is a complete <a href="/spec/#9-extensions-versions-and-conformance">Basic Publisher</a>
          (<a href="#publish-your-first-event">see publishing steps</a>).
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can the JSON file live on a different domain from the human event page?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. If discovery starts from the HTML page’s <code>&lt;link rel="alternate"&gt;</code>
          tag, that establishes the fetched page’s discovery link to the JSON endpoint (and emits a
          relationship warning if the JSON’s declared <code>url</code> does not match). It does not
          verify organizer identity or endorsement (<a href="#refresh-and-provenance">see provenance rules</a>).
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Why does the JSCalendar converter decline some valid OpenAjang events?</summary>
        <p>
          Date-only events, timed events without <code>ends_at</code>, and <code>postponed</code>
          events cannot be represented in JSCalendar without inventing clock hours, durations, or
          active dates, so the adapter returns <code>result: null</code> with a diagnostic
          (<a href="/conversions/#2-jscalendar">see JSCalendar conversion rules</a>).
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What should I do if my event sells out?</summary>
        <p>
          Keep the event’s top-level <code>status</code> as <code>"scheduled"</code> (or
          <code>"rescheduled"</code>). Sold-out is a ticket or registration state, not an event
          lifecycle status: update <code>registration.status</code> to <code>"closed"</code> or
          <code>"waitlist"</code>, or set the relevant ticket’s <code>availability</code> to
          <code>"sold_out"</code>, and advance <code>updated_at</code>.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does publishing an OpenAjang file change the license of my event description or images?</summary>
        <p>
          No. While the OpenAjang specification is licensed under CC BY 4.0 and the repository code
          under Apache-2.0, event descriptions, images, and other material published by organizers
          keep their own terms.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
